        {
          "@value": "`Δ9-THCB` is the owner's search term, registered on 2026-11-11 (ISS-20261111-TAX-041) as one of three distinct search-topic concepts that replace the generic chemical interpretation of `THCB`; `compound_thcb` remains the separate registration of the literal string `THCB`. The term is registered as a search string, not as a substance identification. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED. The cited sources document the compound: Linciano et al. 2020 (DOI 10.1021/acs.jnatprod.9b00876) isolated Δ9-THCB, the butyl homologue of Δ9-THC, from a medicinal Cannabis sativa variety and confirmed its structure and absolute configuration by comparison with a stereoselective synthesis; Tanaka & Kikura-Hanajiri 2024 (DOI 10.1248/yakushi.24-00029) identified Δ9-THCB, and separately Δ8-THCB, among Δ8/Δ9 THC analogues in five oil products distributed online in Japan from 2022 to 2023. Those statements concern analysed substances and products, not the content of any Google Trends or YouTube result and not any product generally. This concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: this change did not verify or select a single external record for the registered string, and the owner's confirmation fixes the query topic, not result-level substance identity. It stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so the placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, structure, synonym or regulatory status. No skos:related link to compound_thcb, compound_delta8_thcb, compound_thc or another new concept is asserted; the Δ8/Δ9 positional-isomer distinction is recorded in this note and in the provenance ledger only. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `Δ9-THCB` / `Δ9-THCB リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; a matching result is not evidence of Δ9-THCB content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a verified external identifier for the named isomer (a documented chemical name with a verified identifier), after which the category that evidence supports can be assigned; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
          "@language": "en"
