        {
          "@value": "`9(R)-HHC` is the owner's search term, registered on 2026-11-15 (ISS-20261115-TAX-045) as one of the 27 forms the owner enumerated for registration from the study's candidate-form inventory in `datasets/cannabinoid-raw-collection`. The term is a registered search string, not a substance identification: the single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED. The evidence recorded for this form in the study inventory is a registry record together with the WHO critical review: PubChem CID 11067065, CAS 36403-90-4 and InChIKey XKRHRBJLCLXSGE-DJIMGWMZSA-N, and the WHO 47th ECDD (2024) §2C, which records (6aR,9R,10aR)-HHC, also called 9β-HHC, as one of the two main epimers of CBD-derived product (ISS-20261111-TAX-042). That is a review statement about product composition, not an identification of any specific product, not this study's analysis and not a claim about any result. The registry record was verified by the earlier review, not by this change. No identifier property and no `skos:exactMatch` are assigned to this taxonomy concept: any CAS / PubChem / UNII / ChEBI / Wikidata / IUPAC values quoted in this note are recorded evidence in `docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md`, were not re-verified in this change, and were not selected for the registered query. It stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so this placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, structure, synonym, pharmacology, safety or regulatory status, and it asserts no relation to the inventory parent term `HHC`: that grouping in the candidate table organised the rows and is not a structural or synonym claim. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `9(R)-HHC` / `9(R)-HHC リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; a matching result is not evidence of this stereochemical configuration content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a verified external record selected f